# tools/al/ales_stereo_crossfade_witness.rish — stereo_crossfade: the equal-power crossfade carried into stereo with ONE SHARED LAW, both channels in lockstep, validated once before either mutates (ALES131).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES131.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-031427_fill-ales131-lotus-stereo-crossfade.md
# Stands on: lotus/crossfade.rye (ALES12 — the mono equal-power crossfade run once per channel, itself reusing ALES11's split and ALES2's one true saturate), lotus/pan.rye (ALES10 — the StereoClip, two
# mono Clips left/right, and its balance invariant), lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 — the Clip, its buf/len/samples, and the EditError reused whole). A Lotus master is a StereoClip whose defining invariant
# is that left and right hold the same length. ALES130's fade closed on the recognition that a stereo fade-out laid under a stereo fade-in IS a stereo crossfade; this rung makes that gesture its own
# primitive: run ALES12's proven mono crossfade on each channel with the SAME equal-power weights. Those weights are positional (identical at each seat regardless of channel), so — exactly as ALES130's
# shared ramp — the shared law preserves the stereo image for free (no measurement, no linking needed).
#
# lotus/stereo_crossfade.rye validates the overlap once against the shared length before either output channel is written (BadRange on a length mismatch between the two masters or an overlap shorter than
# two samples), then crosses both channels through ALES12's crossfade; EditError reused whole. The crux is four laws. THE STEREO CROSSFADE LAW: each channel equals ALES12's mono crossfade byte for byte,
# head pure outgoing and tail pure incoming. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW: out.left.len == out.right.len == the overlap length. THE SHARED-WEIGHT IMAGE LAW: the same weights cross both channels at each seat,
# the seam splicing seamlessly on both speakers and an identical-channel pair staying identical. THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: a mismatch or a too-short overlap refuses BadRange with the output
# untouched, a loud overlap saturating on both channels.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/al/ales_stereo_crossfade_witness.rish

say "ales-stereo-crossfade: Language EN — stereo_crossfade crosses two stereo masters over their shared overlap into an output master, running the same equal-power law on each channel, so two whole masters cross without a dip and without splitting the image. A Lotus master is two Clips heard together, one per speaker, whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length so the two channels stay aligned in time. ALES130's fade closed on the recognition that a stereo fade-out laid under a stereo fade-in is a stereo crossfade; this rung makes that gesture its own primitive, carrying ALES12's proven mono crossfade into stereo. The equal-power weights are positional — identical at each seat regardless of channel — so, exactly as the fade's shared ramp, the shared weights preserve the stereo image for free, and the overlap is validated once against the shared length before either output channel is written so a refusal never writes one channel and leaves the other stale."
say "ales-stereo-crossfade: Style Radiant — the rule is stated positively: refuse BadRange when the two masters name different overlap lengths, and BadRange when the overlap is shorter than two samples, before either output channel is written (both masters enter balanced, so the overlap length is checked once against the shared length up front), else cross both channels over the overlap through ALES12's crossfade with the same equal-power weights on each. It reuses ALES12's EditError by name and ALES12's crossfade for the arithmetic, inventing no new fault and no new primitive. The crux stands out as four laws: the stereo crossfade law (each channel equals ALES12's mono crossfade of that channel's outgoing and incoming windows byte for byte, the head pure outgoing and the tail pure incoming, per channel), the balance / length law (out.left.len == out.right.len == the overlap length, the output a legal balanced master), the shared-weight image law (the same weights cross both channels at each seat so the seam splices seamlessly on both speakers and an identical-channel pair of masters crosses to an identical-channel output, the image never splitting), and the atomicity / degenerate law (a length mismatch and a too-short overlap each refuse BadRange with the output untouched and no channel half-written, a loud overlap saturating on both channels rather than wrapping)."
say "ales-stereo-crossfade: Lens TAME — four bounded i16 Clips (two masters, two channels each) crossed into two more in lockstep by one shared equal-power law; the balance invariant is asserted on entry for both masters and on exit for the output, the overlap length is bound-checked once before any write, and because that check holds identically for both channels each mono cross is pre-validated to succeed so no refusal can land between the two channel writes; crossfade walks the overlap once per channel, running each scaled sample in a wide i64 so neither the scale nor the sum overflows before ALES2's single saturate, never touching a byte past the overlap; it reuses ALES12's crossfade per channel over its public API and ALES12's EditError whole, inventing no new primitive; the stereo crossfade law is proven against ALES12's own crossfade run per channel, the shared-weight image law proves the equal-power center lands each channel's own level at the same positional weight and an identical-channel pair never splits, the two refusals leave the output byte-for-byte untouched, and a loud overlap saturates on both channels; siloed to lotus/, no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/stereo_crossfade"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/stereo_crossfade.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-stereo-crossfade: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/stereo_crossfade.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-stereo-crossfade: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-stereo-crossfade: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-stereo-crossfade' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-stereo-crossfade: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-stereo-crossfade line"
